item.text:
Accepted new evidence below. No score.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States of America: Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/24/mcs062422-united-states-of-america-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"The IMF said the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act was an important step toward addressing supply-side constraints and increased public capital spending by 2.3 percent of 2022 GDP, with spending on roads, transit, ports, broadband, the electricity grid, and EV charging.","locator":"line 284","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF mission statement with a concrete, dated macro-policy assessment and quantified effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year policy adopted in 2022 with medium-term effects from the Biden administration.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden administration policy enacted during his term; not fully attributable to him alone because Congress passed the law.","contrary_evidence":["2022 inflation remained high and household purchasing power was under pressure."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for the United States","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/07/12/united-states-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-staff-report-and-statement-by-the-520659","claim":"The IMF said that just over two years after the COVID-19 shock, unemployment and other labor underutilization measures had returned to end-2019 levels and output was close to its pre-pandemic trend.","locator":"line 260","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF Article IV report with direct macro assessment and clear date fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes 2022 U.S. conditions within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome under the Biden administration, but not solely attributable to Biden because it reflects inherited pandemic recovery and Fed actions.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation was still elevated and the Fed was tightening aggressively."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: United States 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-united-states-2022_eeb7cbe9-en.html","claim":"The OECD said the U.S. economy rebounded strongly from the pandemic recession and that the administration was reinforcing public welfare through packages investing in infrastructure and the climate transition, while warning that the middle class had been hit by stagnant incomes and rising living costs.","locator":"line 3731","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country survey is a high-quality external macro assessment with explicit policy and distribution commentary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 country survey, directly relevant to the target year and its trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level policy environment under Biden; part outcome, part policy attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The same survey noted strong inflationary pressures and fiscal pressures ahead."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Estimated Budgetary Effects of H.R. 5376, the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"Congressional Budget Office","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58366","claim":"CBO estimated that enacting the Inflation Reduction Act would result in a net decrease in the deficit totaling $90 billion over 2022-2031.","locator":"summary lines 35-37","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CBO cost estimate is the official budget score for the legislation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 legislative assessment of a Biden-backed law.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden administration policy choice and signing-era legislative outcome.","contrary_evidence":["CBO projections do not establish realized household benefits or inflation relief on their own."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Budget and Economic Outlook: 2022 to 2032","publisher":"Congressional Budget Office","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58147","claim":"CBO estimated that the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act would gradually boost real GDP by 0.1 percent by calendar year 2026 and increase productivity over time.","locator":"line 894","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CBO baseline outlook with quantified long-run effect from enacted legislation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Targets the 2022 law and its expected medium-term effects.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to Biden because the law was signed under his administration, but Congress enacted it.","contrary_evidence":["The same outlook also noted higher debt could crowd out private investment and raise interest rates."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gross Domestic Product, Fourth Quarter and Year 2022 (Second Estimate)","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2023-02-23","url":"https://www.bea.gov/news/2023/gross-domestic-product-fourth-quarter-and-year-2022-second-estimate","claim":"BEA reported that real GDP increased 2.1 percent in 2022.","locator":"lines 117-123","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"BEA national accounts release is the primary official GDP series.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures the target year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome during Biden's term; attribution is partial because it reflects inherited conditions, private demand, and Fed policy as well as administration choices.","contrary_evidence":["The same release showed PCE inflation at 6.3 percent and broad price pressures in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Poverty in the United States: 2022","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2023/demo/p60-280.html?os=win","claim":"The Census Bureau reported that the official poverty rate in 2022 was 11.5 percent, while the Supplemental Poverty Measure was 12.4 percent and the SPM child poverty rate more than doubled from 5.2 percent in 2021 to 12.4 percent in 2022.","locator":"lines 206-214","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary household-income and poverty report with explicit 2022 estimates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2023 report on 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Broad 2022 economic outcome under Biden; not fully attributable because the report reflects taxes, transfers, and inflation dynamics beyond presidential control.","contrary_evidence":["Other measures showed the labor market remained strong, so the deterioration was mainly inflation/transfer driven rather than mass job loss."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Consumer Price Index News Release - 2022 M12 Results","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_01122023.htm","claim":"BLS reported that the all items CPI-U increased 6.5 percent over the 12 months ending December 2022.","locator":"lines 226-230 and 387-389","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official price-index release with standard BLS methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures inflation in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Macroeconomic condition under Biden but substantially driven by global supply shocks, demand, and Federal Reserve policy rather than presidential control alone.","contrary_evidence":["Later 2022 policy actions targeted costs, deficits, competition, and energy security."],"lenses":["5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Statement of Janet L. Yellen, Secretary of the Treasury, and Shalanda D. Young, Director of the Office of Management and Budget, on Budget Results for Fiscal Year 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1043","claim":"Treasury said the FY 2022 deficit fell by $1.4 trillion, the largest one-year decrease in the Federal deficit in American history.","locator":"line 605","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official budget results statement with quantified fiscal outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers fiscal year 2022 during Biden's term.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level fiscal outcome under Biden, though much of the decline reflected pandemic relief rolling off.","contrary_evidence":["A large part of the change came from the fading of COVID-era emergency spending rather than new restraint alone."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Inflation Reduction Act Primarily Impacts Top 1 Percent of Taxpayers","publisher":"Tax Policy Center","publication_date":"2022-08-11","url":"https://taxpolicycenter.org/taxvox/inflation-reduction-act-primarily-impacts-top-1-percent-taxpayers","claim":"The Tax Policy Center found the IRA's tax provisions were highly progressive, with taxes rising by $6,060 for households in the top 1 percent and by $41,580 for the top 0.1 percent in 2023.","locator":"line 63","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Respected nonpartisan distributional analysis, but still a model-based estimate rather than observed outcomes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Legislation enacted in 2022 with modeled distributional effects.","ruler_attribution":"Direct attribution to Biden-backed legislation signed in 2022; effects are modeled, not observed.","contrary_evidence":["Distributional estimates do not prove realized welfare gains for all households."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflation Reduction Act: Preliminary Estimates of Budgetary and Macroeconomic Effects","publisher":"Penn Wharton Budget Model","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://budgetmodel.wharton.upenn.edu/p/2022-07-29-inflation-reduction-act/","claim":"PWBM estimated that the Inflation Reduction Act would reduce non-interest cumulative deficits by $248 billion over the budget window and that its impact on inflation would be statistically indistinguishable from zero.","locator":"lines 69-73","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-regarded model-based estimate, but still an ex ante simulation with uncertainty.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 legislative estimate of Biden-backed policy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct legislative attribution to the Biden administration, but model uncertainty remains.","contrary_evidence":["Model results differ from other estimates and do not guarantee realized inflation effects."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden vows to give Fed Chair Powell 'space' to fight inflation","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-05-31","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/biden-and-fed-chair-powell-to-discuss-inflation-crisis-on-tuesday-2831920","claim":"Reuters reported that Biden told Powell he respected the independence of the Federal Reserve and would give the Fed space to fight inflation without political interference.","locator":"lines 407-412","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is a strong contemporaneous news source; the claim is explicit but reported through a White House readout.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and squarely relevant to monetary credibility and macro management.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden behavior toward the Fed during his term.","contrary_evidence":["This does not itself prove he never pressured the Fed in other ways, but it is direct evidence of restraint in this meeting."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Instant View: US July payrolls rise more than expected","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/instant-view-us-july-payrolls-rise-more-than-expected-2865343","claim":"Reuters reported that U.S. nonfarm payrolls increased by 528,000 in July 2022 and that employment rose above its pre-pandemic level while wages continued rising at a steady pace.","locator":"lines 427-429","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on the official jobs release; strong for outcome framing, though syndicated.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 labor-market conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome under Biden, but mainly an outcome claim rather than a direct presidential-action claim.","contrary_evidence":["Strong employment does not by itself establish fair distribution or Biden-specific causation."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Is the U.S. in a recession? Jobs and GDP tell a different story","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-07-08","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/is-the-us-in-a-recession-jobs-and-gdp-tell-a-different-story-2845400","claim":"Reuters reported that despite two quarters of negative GDP growth estimates, job growth remained strong, unemployment was below 4 percent, and the Sahm recession rule showed no sign of a U.S. downturn.","locator":"lines 410-457","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters synthesis of market and labor data, useful for recession-risk context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 economic context within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Macro condition under Biden's term; not a direct presidential decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same story noted personal consumption and real disposable income had fallen on an inflation-adjusted basis."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Commerce Department Requests Information on Supporting a Strong U.S. Semiconductor Industry","publisher":"U.S. Department of Commerce","publication_date":"2022-01-24","url":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/press-releases/2022/01/commerce-department-requests-information-supporting-strong-us","claim":"Commerce said it was seeking information to design programs to incentivize investment in semiconductor manufacturing, shared infrastructure for research and prototyping, and related research, explicitly aiming to support a strong domestic semiconductor industry.","locator":"lines 306-311","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official administration implementation record with precise program-design language and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and part of Biden's industrial-policy agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden-administration policy choice through Commerce.","contrary_evidence":["The same document acknowledged immediate shortages and a long-term supply-chain vulnerability."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden-Harris Administration Announces CHIPS for America Leadership","publisher":"U.S. Department of Commerce","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/press-releases/2022/09/biden-harris-administration-announces-chips-america-leadership","claim":"Commerce announced CHIPS for America leadership and said the hires would have responsibility for implementing the CHIPS and Science Act's historic $50 billion semiconductor investment.","locator":"line 307","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official implementation announcement with named governance structure and responsibility.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 implementation of a Biden-backed industrial policy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden-administration implementation under his authority.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation setup does not by itself prove economic success or fair distribution."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.6","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Year in Review: One-Year Anniversary of President Biden’s Competition Executive Order","publisher":"U.S. Department of Commerce","publication_date":"2022-07-11","url":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/blog/2022/07/year-review-one-year-anniversary-president-bidens-competition-executive-order","claim":"Commerce said the administration's competition effort had withdrawn the 2019 standards-essential patents policy statement, prioritized inclusive innovation, and worked across government to strengthen competition and support small businesses and inventors.","locator":"lines 301-314","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official summary of competition-policy implementation, though it is self-evaluative and should be weighed with outside analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 administration policy review directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden-administration competition policy under his authority.","contrary_evidence":["The piece is self-congratulatory and does not independently measure consumer or worker outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Seizing the U.S. infrastructure opportunity: Investing in current and future workers","publisher":"Brookings","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/infrastructure-workforce/","claim":"Brookings argued that infrastructure investment can stimulate economic growth and unleash job creation nationally, and that IIJA, IRA, and CHIPS together were sending hundreds of billions toward new projects and jobs.","locator":"lines 17-19 and 29-30","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent policy analysis from a reputable think tank; still evaluative rather than observed outcome data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 analysis of Biden-era legislation and its expected effects.","ruler_attribution":"Policy package enacted under Biden, though future outcomes depend on implementation and labor-market conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The same analysis warned that workforce shortages could limit gains if not addressed."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Harnessing the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act to train the next generation of workers","publisher":"Brookings","publication_date":"2022-02-23","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/harnessing-the-infrastructure-investment-and-jobs-act-to-train-the-next-generation-of-workers/","claim":"Brookings said the IIJA offered an unprecedented opportunity to accelerate higher-wage careers and expand opportunities for workers, including the unemployed, underemployed, younger students, and adult learners.","locator":"lines 161-163","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent analysis that ties the law to workforce and wage opportunity, but remains prospective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 law and its expected effects in the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden-backed enacted infrastructure policy.","contrary_evidence":["The article frames this as an opportunity, not a guaranteed realized outcome."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"How state and local leaders can harness new infrastructure funding to build a stronger, more inclusive workforce","publisher":"Brookings","publication_date":"2023-01-19","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/how-state-and-local-leaders-can-harness-new-infrastructure-funding-to-build-a-stronger-more-inclusive-workforce/","claim":"Brookings described a once-in-a-generation window to invest in infrastructure and expand economic opportunity, noting that the federal government had directed unprecedented funding to transportation, water, energy, broadband, and climate needs.","locator":"lines 217-219","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent analysis with direct language on the size and opportunity of the federal infrastructure push.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective analysis of the 2021-2022 infrastructure funding window.","ruler_attribution":"Biden-era federal policy package, but implementation responsibility is shared with state and local governments.","contrary_evidence":["The same analysis emphasized that state and local execution would determine whether opportunity was realized."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by Secretary of the Treasury Janet L. Yellen on the Passage of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-08-12","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy0920","claim":"Treasury said the Inflation Reduction Act would make America more competitive, lower health and energy costs for families, create high-quality jobs, bolster energy security, sharply lower emissions, reduce the budget deficit, and increase long-term economic growth.","locator":"lines 610-614","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official administration statement on the enacted law and its intended economic effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, immediately after passage of a Biden-backed law.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden administration policy and signing-era agenda, though this is an intent/benefits statement rather than an outcome measure.","contrary_evidence":["Official intent statements can overstate likely benefits; independent estimates are needed for calibration."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.5","5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement of Administration Policy: H.R. 5376 - Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-06","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-administration-policy-hr-5376-inflation-reduction-act-2022","claim":"The administration strongly supported the IRA because it would lower health care, prescription drug, and energy costs, invest in energy security, make the tax code fairer, and reduce the deficit while fighting inflation.","locator":"lines 63-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct statement of administration policy on a major enacted law; official but self-descriptive.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and tied to Biden's legislative agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden administration position on the legislation.","contrary_evidence":["This is an advocacy document, so external corroboration is required for outcome claims."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BY THE NUMBERS: The Inflation Reduction Act","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-15","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/the-numbers-the-inflation-reduction-act","claim":"Biden said the IRA would cap insulin at $35 per month for Medicare beneficiaries, save 13 million Americans an average of $800 per year on premiums, impose a 15 percent minimum tax on corporate profits, and leave no family making less than $400,000 with a tax increase.","locator":"lines 68-77 and 108-114","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary administration explanation of the enacted policy's distribution and cost features; self-presenting but specific.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in the target year after enactment of a Biden-backed law.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden administration policy framing.","contrary_evidence":["These are projected policy effects, not independently observed outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order 14082—Implementation of the Energy and Infrastructure Provisions of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-09-12","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-14082-implementation-the-energy-and-infrastructure-provisions-the","claim":"The executive order described the IRA as the single largest and most ambitious investment in the U.S. ability to advance clean energy, cut consumer energy costs, promote environmental justice, strengthen energy security, reduce the deficit, and grow the economy.","locator":"line 63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary executive order text, though it is largely declarative and promissory in nature.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 presidential action implementing the IRA.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential action by Biden.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation promises do not prove realized economic gains in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
Search ledger:
- Candidates found: about 62 plausible documents.
- Documents opened: 36 accessible documents.
- Duplicates collapsed: 18.
- Irrelevant/out-of-period: 8.
- Inaccessible/internal-error items: 6.
- Accepted distinct URLs: 24.

Material gaps and next best directions:
- Direct 2022 evidence on 5B.2 remains thinner than the policy and outcome record. Best next source family: Treasury/NEC personnel and cabinet-implementation documents, plus contemporaneous reporting on Yellen, Brainard, Powell, and OMB/CEA staffing decisions.
- Household distribution still needs more depth across race, region, and income tiers. Best next source family: Census appendix tables, CBO distribution tables, and more TPC/PWBM distributional analyses tied to 2022 outcomes.
- Inflation and real-wage pain are well evidenced, but the causal split between Biden policy and inherited/global shocks is still mixed. Best next source family: IMF/OECD plus Fed and BLS longitudinal wage series, ideally with explicit commentary on Biden-era fiscal/industrial policy.
- Competition / anti-capture evidence is decent on policy design, but weaker on realized market effects. Best next source family: FTC/DOJ enforcement outcomes, merger challenges, noncompete work, and independent assessments of consumer-price or wage effects.
